1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,483
A red shrimp can look almost black in the
water where it lives.

2
00:00:03,483 --> 00:00:08,257
Bring in white ROV lights and the same
animal suddenly turns bright red.

3
00:00:08,257 --> 00:00:11,573
The color did not change. The available
light did.

4
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:13,765
Why many deep sea creatures are red?

5
00:00:13,765 --> 00:00:17,073
Color only works when matching light
reaches it.

6
00:00:17,073 --> 00:00:22,654
Seawater removes red wavelengths early, so
a red body in deep blue light reflects

7
00:00:22,654 --> 00:00:25,824
very little visible light back to an
observer.

8
00:00:25,824 --> 00:00:30,435
The rest of this video follows that
question through the actual environmental

9
00:00:30,435 --> 00:00:35,226
constraints described by NOAA, MBARI,
Smithsonian, and Woods Hole sources in the

10
00:00:35,226 --> 00:00:36,304
companion article.

11
00:00:36,304 --> 00:00:40,096
What surprised me is that red is not a
warning color down there.

12
00:00:40,096 --> 00:00:44,909
Under the light that actually reaches the
animal, red can function more like black

13
00:00:44,909 --> 00:00:45,555
camouflage.

14
00:00:45,555 --> 00:00:50,357
An object looks red near the surface
because red wavelengths strike it and are

15
00:00:50,357 --> 00:00:51,896
reflected to an observer.

16
00:00:51,896 --> 00:00:56,231
Deeper down, those wavelengths have
already been absorbed by the water.

17
00:00:56,231 --> 00:01:00,871
Without red illumination, red tissue
returns almost no visible red light and

18
00:01:00,871 --> 00:01:02,397
looks dark gray or black.

19
00:01:02,397 --> 00:01:06,395
In dim midwater, avoiding a detectable
silhouette is critical.

20
00:01:06,395 --> 00:01:11,166
Red or black skin helps an animal blend
into the background when viewed by

21
00:01:11,166 --> 00:01:14,712
predators or prey using the available
blue-green light.

22
00:01:14,712 --> 00:01:21,781
Some animals combine red external tissue
with a red stomach lining that hides the

23
00:01:21,781 --> 00:01:25,533
glow of recently eaten bioluminescent
prey.

24
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:28,084
Ocean color patterns shift gradually.

25
00:01:28,084 --> 00:01:32,152
Near the surface, blue and silver
countershading is common.

26
00:01:32,152 --> 00:01:34,771
Deeper, transparency becomes useful.

27
00:01:34,771 --> 00:01:38,699
Farther down, red and black bodies become
more common.

28
00:01:38,699 --> 00:01:42,094
These are broad patterns, not rigid rules.

29
00:01:42,094 --> 00:01:46,621
Habitat, behavior and visual systems vary
among species.

30
00:01:46,621 --> 00:01:51,640
A remotely operated vehicle carries bright
white lights that include red wavelengths.

31
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:56,776
The camera therefore records vivid crimson
bodies that would look nearly black under

32
00:01:56,776 --> 00:01:58,611
natural deep-sea illumination.

33
00:01:58,611 --> 00:02:03,874
That is why expedition footage can show an
apparently bright animal in an environment

34
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:06,413
where the color functions as concealment.

35
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:09,703
A common question is: Can deep-sea animals
see red?

36
00:02:09,703 --> 00:02:14,477
Many deep-sea visual systems are most
sensitive to blue-green wavelengths.

37
00:02:14,477 --> 00:02:19,251
Sensitivity varies, and some species have
specialized visual capabilities.

38
00:02:19,251 --> 00:02:24,150
A common question is: Why do red deep-sea
animals look bright in video?

39
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:28,635
ROV lights contain red wavelengths that
natural deep water lacks.

40
00:02:28,635 --> 00:02:33,931
A common question is: Are all deep-sea
animals red? No.

41
00:02:33,931 --> 00:02:41,056
Black, transparent, silver, pale and
bioluminescent forms are also common.

42
00:02:41,056 --> 00:02:47,628
A common question is: Is red coloration
the same as bioluminescence? No.

43
00:02:47,628 --> 00:02:54,565
Pigment controls reflected light;
bioluminescence produces light chemically.

44
00:02:54,565 --> 00:02:56,347
Color is reflected light.

45
00:02:56,347 --> 00:03:00,907
At the surface, a red body reflects red
wavelengths to our eyes.

46
00:03:00,907 --> 00:03:04,726
As those wavelengths are absorbed by
seawater, there is less red light

47
00:03:04,726 --> 00:03:05,872
available to reflect.

48
00:03:05,872 --> 00:03:11,843
NOAA's educational page says red light
does not penetrate at about 100 meters,

49
00:03:11,843 --> 00:03:14,293
while blue travels much farther.

50
00:03:14,293 --> 00:03:17,083
That makes red and black useful midwater
colors.

51
00:03:17,083 --> 00:03:21,558
The dramatic red animals in expedition
footage are often being illuminated by

52
00:03:21,558 --> 00:03:25,509
white lamps that restore wavelengths
absent from their normal world.

53
00:03:25,509 --> 00:03:29,184
Imagine lowering a row of colored cards
through clear water.

54
00:03:29,184 --> 00:03:32,245
The cards do not carry their appearance
with them.

55
00:03:32,245 --> 00:03:36,603
Their appearance depends on which
wavelengths remain available to reflect.

56
00:03:36,603 --> 00:03:41,196
Red wavelengths are absorbed relatively
quickly, so a red body loses the light

57
00:03:41,196 --> 00:03:42,845
that would make it look red.

58
00:03:42,845 --> 00:03:47,853
Under the blue-green light that penetrates
deeper, that same body can appear dark.

59
00:03:47,853 --> 00:03:50,095
The animal has not changed pigment.

60
00:03:50,095 --> 00:03:55,026
The lighting environment has changed the
information available to a viewer or

61
00:03:55,026 --> 00:03:55,603
predator.

62
00:03:55,603 --> 00:03:58,466
This is why ROV footage needs
interpretation.

63
00:03:58,466 --> 00:04:03,619
White lamps bring a broad spectrum into a
place where it normally does not exist.

64
00:04:03,619 --> 00:04:08,020
A shrimp that looks scarlet on camera may
look nearly black under ambient

65
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:08,683
conditions.

66
00:04:08,683 --> 00:04:13,160
The light makes the footage useful for
identification, but it can mislead the

67
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:14,672
audience about camouflage.

68
00:04:14,672 --> 00:04:17,329
Depth is also not a single switch.

69
00:04:17,329 --> 00:04:23,190
Water clarity, particles, angle, weather,
and local conditions affect light

70
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:24,128
penetration.

71
00:04:24,128 --> 00:04:28,592
Red coloration can be useful in one depth
range and irrelevant in another.

72
00:04:28,592 --> 00:04:33,059
Some animals combine pigment with
transparency, counterillumination,

73
00:04:33,059 --> 00:04:35,096
reflective tissue, or behavior.

74
00:04:35,096 --> 00:04:40,483
The strongest version of this script does
not claim that every red deep-sea animal

75
00:04:40,483 --> 00:04:42,848
evolved for exactly the same reason.

76
00:04:42,848 --> 00:04:47,996
It shows the physics, then asks whether
the documented habitat and behavior fit

77
00:04:47,996 --> 00:04:50,864
the camouflage explanation for that
species.

78
00:04:50,864 --> 00:04:55,382
Every red-creature shot should carry a
small note when artificial white light is

79
00:04:55,382 --> 00:04:56,512
doing the revealing.

80
00:04:56,512 --> 00:05:01,179
Without that note, the footage quietly
teaches the opposite of the biology.

81
00:05:01,179 --> 00:05:06,670
The audience sees a vivid red animal and
hears that red is camouflage, which can

82
00:05:06,670 --> 00:05:08,043
sound contradictory.

83
00:05:08,043 --> 00:05:12,787
The correction is visual: show the same
body under a simplified deep-water light

84
00:05:12,787 --> 00:05:14,981
spectrum, then restore the ROV lamps.

85
00:05:14,981 --> 00:05:19,654
That lets viewers watch the color change
as an effect of illumination rather than

86
00:05:19,654 --> 00:05:21,269
accept it as a verbal claim.

87
00:05:21,269 --> 00:05:25,979
It also gives the editor a repeatable
technique for future color episodes.

88
00:05:25,979 --> 00:05:30,914
Whenever a video depends on what an animal
looks like, ask what light produced the

89
00:05:30,914 --> 00:05:31,275
image.

90
00:05:31,275 --> 00:05:33,953
Color is not a fixed label attached to a
body.

91
00:05:33,953 --> 00:05:38,437
It is an interaction among pigment,
available wavelengths, viewing angle, and

92
00:05:38,437 --> 00:05:39,835
the sensor recording it.

93
00:05:39,835 --> 00:05:45,218
I will not say that red pigment always
evolved for camouflage or that every red

94
00:05:45,218 --> 00:05:47,808
animal is invisible at the same depth.

95
00:05:47,808 --> 00:05:51,348
Pigment can have multiple functions, and
light fields vary.

96
00:05:51,348 --> 00:05:56,087
The supported claim is narrower: because
red wavelengths disappear quickly, red

97
00:05:56,087 --> 00:06:00,827
tissue can return little available light
and appear dark in many deep settings.

98
00:06:00,827 --> 00:06:06,192
The species, depth, and behavior decide
whether that physical advantage is

99
00:06:06,192 --> 00:06:07,787
biologically relevant.

100
00:06:07,787 --> 00:06:12,897
Pause every time the narrator says red,
black, or invisible and ask whether the

101
00:06:12,897 --> 00:06:17,232
current shot uses natural ambient light or
artificial illumination.

102
00:06:17,232 --> 00:06:21,002
If it uses white ROV light, the caption
must say so.

103
00:06:21,002 --> 00:06:26,946
If depth is uncertain, do not use the shot
as proof of a depth-specific camouflage

104
00:06:26,946 --> 00:06:27,381
claim.

105
00:06:27,381 --> 00:06:32,466
The audience should be able to see why the
same animal changes appearance instead of

106
00:06:32,466 --> 00:06:36,219
being asked to trust a color fact that
contradicts the screen.

107
00:06:36,219 --> 00:06:40,809
This check also prevents a beautiful deck
photograph from being treated as a

108
00:06:40,809 --> 00:06:43,829
faithful view of the animal's normal
visual world.

109
00:06:43,829 --> 00:06:48,253
This check also prevents a beautiful deck
photograph from being treated as a

110
00:06:48,253 --> 00:06:51,163
faithful view of the animal's normal
visual world.

111
00:06:51,163 --> 00:06:53,866
Treat the episode like a lighting
experiment.

112
00:06:53,866 --> 00:06:58,552
Show the same original illustration under
full-spectrum light, then remove red

113
00:06:58,552 --> 00:07:00,715
wavelengths and let the body darken.

114
00:07:00,715 --> 00:07:02,860
Follow with an ROV-light reveal.

115
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:08,357
Do not use unlicensed expedition footage
simply because it demonstrates the effect

116
00:07:08,357 --> 00:07:13,854
well; the original diagram and admitted
media must carry the explanation unless an

117
00:07:13,854 --> 00:07:16,603
external clip passes the rights manifest.

118
00:07:16,603 --> 00:07:21,716
The depth at which a color disappears
varies with water clarity, particles, sun

119
00:07:21,716 --> 00:07:23,723
angle, and the amount of light.

120
00:07:23,723 --> 00:07:29,834
'Red disappears at 100 meters' is an
educational approximation, not a universal

121
00:07:29,834 --> 00:07:30,608
hard line.

122
00:07:30,608 --> 00:07:36,787
Red coloration is also not used by every
deep animal and does not make an animal

123
00:07:36,787 --> 00:07:38,332
perfectly invisible.

124
00:07:38,332 --> 00:07:44,279
Bioluminescent predators, close-range
vision, silhouette, movement, and other

125
00:07:44,279 --> 00:07:45,824
senses still matter.

126
00:07:45,824 --> 00:07:48,747
Red deep-sea animals are not dressed for
our lights.

127
00:07:48,747 --> 00:07:51,952
They are dressed for the wavelengths their
world removes.

128
00:07:51,952 --> 00:07:56,611
The next time an ROV reveals a brilliant
scarlet squid or shrimp, remember that the

129
00:07:56,611 --> 00:07:58,688
camera brought the red light with it.
